From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FE Fates port <engine@localhost>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] core: Fire Emblem Fates randomizer: label pointers in person
 files (laid in memory after the game stores the file)

Round 74 (docs/ROUND74_PLAN.md A3, docs/rando/FULL_BLOCK.md section 2, owner
OK "Yes, start round 74"): a chapter person file (GameData/Person/**) whose
cells include label pointers (p lines: the full swap's aid, mpid_name and
mpid_help of the HANDOVER rows and the chapter copies, written only for a run
made with look = full) is no longer kept original. It takes the in-memory path
placement files with label pointers take since round 58: never patched in the
read; found in the Person archive list by its name (patch 0018) once the game
stored it at a new data start, then every cell of the file (its v cells too)
checked against the old values / old label text and laid at the first system
call, all or nothing (fates_rando::apply_file: g targets from GameData's data
start, s targets from the person file's own data start). A guard that fails
keeps the file original with a notice, as before.

The decision is now one function, fates_rando_session::first_way(kind,
has_ptrs) -> Way::{InRead, Late, Refused}, with late_ok(kind) for the
too-big fallback. Map settings files with label pointers are still refused.

Off means original: a person file without p cells (every run the owner's
page can make, every run made before round 74) is patched in the read
exactly as before; no new code runs for it.

New tests person_file_label_pointers_are_laid_in_memory_all_or_nothing
(fates_rando: g, s and v cells on a made-up person file found in the Person
list, a failing guard writes nothing, revert puts every cell back) and
person_files_with_label_pointers_take_the_in_memory_way (fates_rando_session).
